Story location metadata: overall='medieval town'; starting="the tavern's wooden floor"; setting='The tavern is a rectangular stone room measuring thirty feet from north to south and twenty feet east to west. The northern wall opens onto the main doorway, eight feet wide at its center; the southern wall has a six‑foot back door centered with a wooden latch. A polished wooden counter runs north–south along a line five feet east of the western wall and three feet west of the eastern wall, extending twelve feet in length. At the northern end of this counter sits a low stone hearth, six by two feet wide, centered on the counter’s edge.\n\nDirectly ahead of the counter is a short bar area eight feet long along the north side and four feet deep; atop it rests a high bar stool on a one‑foot raised platform. Above the bar section hangs a crystal shelf six feet wide and two feet tall, mounted seven feet from the floor to hold cups. Three stools line the northern edge of this bar, spaced evenly every 2½ feet.\n\nAcross the room east–west are five round tables each four feet in diameter: two on the eastern side at six feet from that wall, one central table ten feet from both walls, and two on the western side also six feet from their wall. Each table has a lantern hanging directly above it, six feet high. A long rectangular back table runs along the southern wall, centered between the back door and the eastern corner; a lantern hangs over this table as well.\n\nThe wooden floorboards creak underfoot, and the stone walls give the space an unmistakable medieval feel. Lanterns cast warm light across tables and bar stools, while the polished counter gleams beside the low hearth where ale barrels rest. The room’s layout—central counter, back door, bar section, crystal shelf, round tables with lanterns, and a back table—provides ample seating for patrons in this stone‑built tavern.'; location_state={'location': {'name': 'Stone‑Built Tavern', 'description': 'A medieval tavern inside a